  "fontScale": 1.25,
  "intro": "The Marathi script: vowels and vowel signs, the consonants (with Marathi's own sounds), special signs and conjuncts, numerals, the main reading rules, and a key to the symbols in the pronunciations.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How the Marathi script works",
      "text": [
        "Marathi is written in Devanagari, the same script as Hindi, from left to right with letters hanging from a line along the top. Marathi adds ळ and a few signs for English words (ॲ, ऑ).",
        "Each consonant carries a built-in vowel, अ [ə]: क on its own is kə. Other vowels after a consonant are shown with a vowel sign; a vowel at the start of a word uses its full letter. Two consonants with no vowel between them join into a conjunct: स + त = स्त.",
        "If you can read Hindi, watch for the differences: च and ज are often said ts and dz, ण is a real retroflex n, ळ is its own letter, and ज्ञ is \"dnya\"."
      ]
    },
